  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`, a procedural floating-islands sunset scene. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run `vite build` or render a frame, so the look and frame rate are unchecked.\n\n- **Islands:** 11 islands of different sizes. Each has a ragged inverted-cone rock underside and a domed grass top, both built with `geometry.define`. Trees are instanced cones on cylinder trunks, and crystals are instanced emissive shards. Each island bobs with its own speed, phase and amplitude, plus a slight sway.\n- **Sky and sun:** `sky.gradient` blends orange at the horizon into pink and purple overhead. There's a large low emissive sun sphere near the horizon, with two directional lights and bloom.\n- **Clouds:** 15 cloud banks at depths from about -40 to -320. Each is one instanced mesh of squashed spheres that drifts at its own speed and wraps around. One fixed bank sits at the base of the waterfall as mist.\n- **Waterfall:** a tapered four-box stream hangs from the largest island, with 44 animated droplets that shrink as they fall.\n- **Haze:** distant islands and clouds are tinted pale blue-lavender or pink by their distance from the camera. I did this by colour instead of using fog.\n- **Camera:** it moves on an ellipse with a 48-second loop through `app.camera.setPose`, and takes no user input.\n- **Contract:** the app draws into `#app`, runs on `requestAnimationFrame`, and sets `window.__EVAL_READY__` after the first frame.\n\nThe sky gradient, the emissive sun, the water opacity and the bloom strength are my guesses at how the engine renders them. If the first capture looks off, those are the values to tune.\n\n",
